# The line-ending policy lives HERE rather than in each machine's config. With
# no .gitattributes the behaviour is whatever Git was installed with: the
# Windows installer sets core.autocrlf=true system-wide, which stores LF in the
# index but writes CRLF into the working tree.
#
# That split is what made generated files show as modified with an EMPTY diff.
# Tauri's Android codegen, Gradle and the node scripts all write LF; Git wanted
# CRLF; the mismatch was never a real change but was enough to block a branch
# switch ("local changes would be overwritten"). Pinning the working tree to LF
# makes Git agree with what the tooling actually writes.
#
# The index has always been LF — all 191 text files — so this rewrites no
# history. It only changes what lands on disk.
* text=auto eol=lf

# cmd.exe is the one consumer that genuinely wants CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# `gradlew` is a POSIX shell script, and CRLF there is a runtime failure rather
# than a cosmetic diff: sh reads the shebang as "/bin/sh\r" and reports
# "bad interpreter". It was checked out CRLF before this file existed. The rule
# above already covers it; it is named because it is the case that actually
# breaks.
src-tauri/gen/android/gradlew text eol=lf

# Assets Git must never rewrite. text=auto already detects these by content,
# but naming them stops a future file that happens to contain no NUL byte from
# being mangled as text.
*.glb   binary
*.icns  binary
*.ico   binary
*.jar   binary
*.png   binary
*.webp  binary
*.wasm  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.onnx  binary
